```cpp
/**
 * @brief Initializes a spatial partitioning octree for efficient collision detection in 3D simulations.
 *
 * The octree dynamically resizes its nodes based on the number of objects being added, and supports multiple levels of octrees for optimized performance in different density areas.
 *
 * @param featureSize The resolution (size) of each node in the octree.
 * @param initialLeafNodes Number of leaf nodes initially allocated.
 * @param maxDataCapacity Maximum number of objects that a single node can hold before it is split.
 * @param[out] root Pointer to the root node of the octree.
 */
void initializeOctree(float featureSize, int initialLeafNodes, int maxDataCapacity, OctreeNode** root) {
    if (!root) {
        throw std::invalid_argument("Root pointer is null.");
    }

    *root = new OctreeNode(featureSize, maxDataCapacity);

    if (!(*root)) {
        throw std::bad_alloc();
    }

    // Initialize root node with given initialLeafNodes.
    (*root)->initialize(initialLeafNodes);
}

/**
 * @brief Represents a single node in an octree.
 */
class OctreeNode {
public:
    float featureSize; /**< Size of this node in the simulation space. */
    int maxDataCapacity; /**< Maximum number of objects that can be stored in this node before it is split. */

private:
    std::vector<Object*> objects; /**< Collection of objects that are within this node. */
    OctreeNode* parent; /**< Pointer to the parent node, if any. */
    std::array<OctreeNode*, 8> children; /**< Pointers to the 8 child nodes. */

public:
    OctreeNode(float featureSize, int maxDataCapacity) : featureSize(featureSize), maxDataCapacity(maxDataCapacity) {}

    void initialize(int initialLeafNodes);

    // Methods for adding, removing and querying objects...
};

/**
 * @brief Initializes a new OctreeNode with the given number of leaf nodes.
 */
void OctreeNode::initialize(int initialLeafNodes) {
    if (initialLeafNodes <= 0) {
        throw std::invalid_argument("Initial number of leaf nodes must be greater than zero.");
    }

    objects.reserve(maxDataCapacity); // Reserve memory for objects to avoid reallocation during adding.

    for (int i = 0; i < initialLeafNodes; ++i) {
        if (!children[i]) {
            children[i] = new OctreeNode(featureSize / 2.0f, maxDataCapacity);
        }
        children[i]->initialize(initialLeafNodes / 2);
    }
}
```

This code provides the basis for an octree implementation in C++ that can be integrated into a simulation framework. The `initializeOctree()` function initializes the root node of the octree, and the `OctreeNode` class represents each individual node in the octree hierarchy. The root node is initialized with a given number of leaf nodes and the feature size and maximum data capacity parameters are passed to each child node recursively as they are created.

The `OctreeNode` class also includes a method for managing memory efficiently by reserving space for objects to avoid reallocation during adding. Additionally, error checking has been implemented in the code to handle potential issues such as null pointers and invalid input parameters.

