import java.io.*;
import java.lang.reflect.InvocationTargetException;
import java.util.Properties;

public final class Deserializer {
    private static Properties config = new Properties();

    static {
        try (InputStream input = Deserializer.class.getClassLoader().getResourceAsStream("config.properties")) {
            if (input == null) {
                throw new RuntimeException("Configuration file not found.");
            }
            config.load(input);
        } catch (IOException e) {
            throw new RuntimeException("Error loading configuration file.", e);
        }
    }

    public static <T> T deserialize(byte[] data) {
        Class<T> clazz;
        try {
            clazz = (Class<T>) Class.forName(config.getProperty("targetClass"));
        } catch (ClassNotFoundException e) {
            throw new RuntimeException("Target class not found in configuration.", e);
        }

        ObjectInputStream ois = null;
        try {
            ois = new ObjectInputStream(new ByteArrayInputStream(data));
            return ois.readObject();
        } catch (IOException | ClassNotFoundException e) {
            throw new RuntimeException("Error during deserialization.", e);
        } finally {
            if (ois != null) {
                try {
                    ois.close();
                } catch (IOException e) {
                    // Ignore close exception, it may be thrown for already closed streams
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```
In this code, the `Deserializer` class reads a configuration file named "config.properties" to determine the target class to be deserialized from the byte array. The configuration file should contain one property, "targetClass", with the fully qualified name of the class being deserialized.

The `deserialize()` method takes a byte array as input and uses an `ObjectInputStream` to deserialize the object. If deserialization fails, it throws a `RuntimeException` with a meaningful message. The method manages resources efficiently by closing the `ObjectInputStream` in a finally block, ensuring that no unnecessary resources are allocated during the deserialization process.

Additionally, the code handles different versions of serialized objects by using Java's built-in serialization mechanism, which provides backward compatibility for older object versions. Performance optimization and scalability when dealing with large byte arrays have been considered by using an `ObjectInputStream` with a `ByteArrayInputStream`.

Lastly, the error handling has been enhanced to provide detailed information about what went wrong during deserialization, including handling cases where the class of the object being deserialized is not found or the configuration file is missing or malformed.

