2. Java Method (`MD5Hash.java`)

```java
import java.security.MessageDigest;
import java.math.BigInteger;
import java.nio.ByteBuffer;

public class MD5Hash {
    public static byte[] hash(String input) throws Exception {
        MessageDigest md = MessageDigest.getInstance("MD5");
        byte[] messageDigest = md.digest(input.getBytes());
        BigInteger no = new BigInteger(1, messageDigest);
        String hashtext = no.toString(16);
        while (hashtext.length() < 32) {
            hashtext = "0" + hashtext;
        }
        return hashtext.getBytes();
    }
}
```

Regarding the cipher choice, AES-128 and AES-256 offer good balance between performance and security in a high-concurrency environment. However, AES-128 is less secure due to its shorter key size, making AES-256 a better option when possible. ChaCha20 provides strong security with faster performance than AES but requires more resources for key setup. GCM mode should be used with all of the above ciphers as it provides additional integrity and authenticity protections. For the MD5 hash, it is no longer considered secure due to collision attacks and should only be used for compatibility or legacy purposes. Use SHA-256 or SHA-3 instead when possible.

