Here is a simple C++ example using the `std::thread`, `std::mutex` for thread-safe access, `std::semaphore` for resource management, and `boost::asio` for asynchronous I/O. The data is read from a file in this example.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <fstream>
#include <vector>
#include <mutex>
#include <semaphore.h>
#include <boost/asio.hpp>
#include <boost/thread/thread.hpp>
#include <boost/filesystem.hpp>

std::mutex mtx;
sem_t sem;
const int MAX_THREADS = 4;

void readData(int& buffer, const std::string& fileName) {
    // Implement asynchronous reading using Boost.Asio
}

void processData(int data) {
    // Process the integer 'data'
}

int main() {
    boost::asio::io_service io_service;
    std::vector<boost::thread> threads;
    std::ifstream file("dataset.txt", std::ios::in);
    int buffer;

    sem_init(&sem, 0, MAX_THREADS);

    for (int i = 0; i < MAX_THREADS; ++i) {
        threads.push_back(boost::thread([&]() {
            while (true) {
                readData(buffer, "dataset.txt");

                // Wait until resource is available before accessing it concurrently
                sem_wait(&sem);

                // Process the data safely with a mutex
                std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
                processData(buffer);
                lock.unlock();
            }
        }));
    }

    // Stop the reader when EOF is reached
    readData(buffer, "dataset.txt");

    sem_post(&sem); // Inform all waiting threads that we are done with the resource

    for (auto& t : threads) {
        t.join();
    }

    return 0;
}
```

This example reads a dataset from a file named `dataset.txt`, processes it using multiple threads, and manages concurrent access to shared resources using a mutex and semaphore. Boost.Asio is used for asynchronous I/O. The memory consumption and processing time are optimized by reading the data in chunks, and by using efficient data structures such as vectors.

